  "name": "Question 11",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13938989/assets/111833881cf8e939d896f23a5ab7ca436f9265e7b8fd66c099eb118eab8fd6b0.png)\n\nEstimate the equation of the trend line for the scatter plot above. Round both the slope and the $y$-intercept to the nearest $0.5.$",
  "worked_solution": "First, let's draw an approximate trend line for our scatter plot:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13938989/assets/fdad2c939372e14617ba4e393037d7f5852cd8114467aa914ccb8963c0eacfeb.png)\n\nIt seems that the trend line passes through $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})\u2248(2,5)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})\u2248(6,1).$\n\nWe will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form \n\n$$\ny=mx+b.\n$$\n\nFirst, we estimate the slope $m$ of the line as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & \u2248\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-5}{6-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4}{4} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $m=-1$ into the slope-intercept form above, we get\n\n$$\ny=-x+b.\n$$\n\nWe now need to estimate the $y$-intercept $b.$ We can do this by substituting the coordinates of a point on the line. Any point will do, so let's substitute $(2,5):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-x+b \\\\ 5 & =-2+b \\\\ 7 & =b \\\\ b & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is \n\n$$\ny=-x+7.\n$$",
